---
import Base from '../layouts/base.astro';
import { brand, site } from '../lib/site';
import { publishedPosts, stats, utcDay } from '../lib/trust';

const posts = await publishedPosts('zh');
const s = stats(posts);
---

<Base
  title="About"
  description={`${brand.name} is a public record kept by ${site.author}: dated, sourced, checkable.`}
  path="/about"
  lang="en"
  switchHref="/zh/about">
  <section class="wrap measure flow">
    <h1>About this site, and who runs it</h1>

    <div class="prose">
      <p>
        I am <strong>{site.author}</strong> — {site.role}. This site exists for one purpose: to put what I actually work on,
        think through, and get wrong into public, in date order.
      </p>

      <h2>Why bother</h2>
      <p>
        Models can generate a whole convincing portfolio overnight: cases, CVs, even testimonials. When everything can be
        produced in bulk, the only thing left that proves "a real person has been doing this for a long time" is a
        <strong>continuous record with timestamps that anyone can check</strong>.
      </p>
      <p>
        So this site does not optimise for beauty. It optimises for <strong>not stopping</strong> and for
        <strong>being checkable</strong>. A few hundred words a day, for years, ends up harder than any introduction.
      </p>

      <h2>Who I am</h2>
      <div class="card-flat">
        <p class="meta">
          Written by me, not by an assistant. Until these three are filled in, the page stays honest about being incomplete.
        </p>
        <ul>
          <li>What I work on: the system, which year it started, where it stands, who uses it.</li>
          <li>How I got here: the engineering line, the business line, the two worst mistakes.</li>
          <li>What I am good at, and what I deliberately do not do.</li>
        </ul>
      </div>

      <h2>The rules of this record</h2>
      <ul>
        <li>All timestamps are UTC. Nothing is edited after publication; late additions are marked "addendum".</li>
        <li>Every published entry joins a hash chain over its full body (<a href="/en/verify">how to verify</a>). Edit one character and the head changes.</li>
        <li>No credentials, host addresses, client data, unreleased business numbers, or identity-grade private details.
          A pinyin name plus verifiable anchors is enough; handing over a life is not honesty.</li>
        <li>Static site, no trackers, no cookie banner. Source on GitHub, build on Cloudflare Pages.</li>
      </ul>

      <h2>How to use it</h2>
      <ul>
        <li>Engineering ability: read the <a href="/en/journal">journal</a> — concrete traps and fixes.</li>
        <li>Judgement: read the <a href="/en/essays">essays</a>, including where I was wrong and how I corrected it.</li>
        <li>Whether this is one human maintaining it: the <a href="/en/timeline">timeline</a> and the <a href="/en/verify">record chain</a>.</li>
        <li>Partnership: email {site.anchors.email}, subject line stating what you want.</li>
      </ul>

      <p class="meta">
        Public since {site.start}: {s.entries} entries / {s.activeDays} days with updates · last update
        {s.latest ? utcDay(s.latest) : '—'}
      </p>
    </div>
  </section>
</Base>
